WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.501
-You know our next guest
from his work

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.769
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:06.706
which airs this Saturday
on NBC with host Parl--

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:10.342
excuse me -- host Paul Rudd
and musical guest Charli XCX.

00:00:10.342 --> 00:00:12.711
His new show "Saturday Morning
All Star Hits!"

00:00:12.711 --> 00:00:15.114
is streaming now on Netflix.
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:17.250
-Welcome to "Smash." I'm Skip!

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.018
-And I'm Treybor. And we're...

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:21.754
-[ Echoing ] Lost!

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:23.623
-We got to get back
because we can't miss

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.091
an all-new "Strongimal."

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:28.361
-Yeah, 'cause
I do a voice on it.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:29.996
-Oh, yeah, that's --
that's this week.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:33.333
-Yep, guess the dudes who make
cartoonarooskies

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.770
think my voice
is totally zuh-zi-zas.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.738
-What does that mean?
-Hey, if you have to zas,

00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:42.207
then you'll never zas!

00:00:42.207 --> 00:00:45.211
-Please welcome back to the show
Kyle Mooney, everybody.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.480
Hello, Kyle.
-Yo!

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.882
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:53.686
-Kyle, I say this so sincerely.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.321
I've always felt
like you're someone

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.390
whenever you do something
on "SNL," I feel like,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.493
oh, you have such an authentic
and voice that is true to you.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.895
And yet I have
never seen anything

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:05.264
that is more hyper-cent--

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:08.000
like hyper-specifically
Kyle Mooney than this show.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.770
You must be thrilled
with how it turned out.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.140
-I feel great, and yeah,
that is the thing

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.010
I feel like everybody
is saying, is "This is so you,"

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.112
which is -- I can't tell
how I feel about that

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:23.683
because it's all based
on children's cartoons.

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:27.320
But at the same time, like, yes,
this is the stuff that I love

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.355
and I feel so great about it.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.925
-I mean, it's basically you play
twins who are hosting

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.560
a Saturday morning cartoon block

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.097
and they are '80s and '90s
cartoons that again,

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:40.799
are so -- it's an homage, right?

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:45.405
I mean, it's safe to say these
are the things you love deeply.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:48.908
-So much. I do think
it could be an issue.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:51.544
[ Laughter ]
I mean, like, this is my life.

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.947
This is -- And the whole --

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.215
The show is presented
as if it's taped off TV.

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:58.584
So you're getting, like,
commercials and news clips.

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.487
And I play
all of these characters.

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:02.688
We got to make these cartoons,
but seriously, this is the stuff

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.057
that I feel like
I've been studying for this

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:08.394
since 1988
or something like that.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.429
-Now, was this a case
where you had to go back

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:12.398
and try to remember
what those cartoons were like?

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:15.435
Or have you been watching them
continuously into adulthood?

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:16.603
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:18.804
-No, no, no. I do every day.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:21.441
[ Laughter ]
I have a pretty massive VHS

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:25.044
collection, actually,
and this is --

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:26.445
-This is your VHS.

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:28.914
For people who think you're,
like, lying, these are like,

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:30.816
actually...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:33.720
You have and you will
just have people over

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.789
right, fellow castmates,
friends in New York and say,

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.958
"Hey, you just grab a tape,
my friend."

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:41.694
-Yeah, well, I mean,
like, you and I overlapped.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.829
Do you remember that?
-I do. Yeah, of course.

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:44.998
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:47.934
It was great, by the way
just to be around you.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:51.036
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.036 --> 00:02:52.572
But yeah, my first year,
I don't know.

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:56.709
How would you describe
"Saturday Night Live" as a job?

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.311
Hectic, stressful, I don't know.
What are the -- How do you...?

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:02.181
-Well, yeah, I think those
are really accurate,

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:04.216
and then especially starting

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:07.386
at "SNL" is the worst haunting
feeling in the world

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.087
because you have a day
where you think

00:03:09.087 --> 00:03:12.692
"I've joined comedy royalty,"
and then your second day,

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.127
you're like, "They're going to
cut my head off."

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.861
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:18.898
-Yeah, I love saying
that too, just the whole

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:21.266
"joining comedy royalty" line.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.266 --> 00:03:24.604
But you're right. It only lasts
for 24 hours.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:25.905
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:29.909
But yeah, no.
Like, my first year

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.044
after the after parties
on Saturday nights

00:03:32.044 --> 00:03:36.982
at like, you know, 4:30, 5:30
in the morning,

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:38.718
I would typically have
some friends

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:43.956
from the show come over
and and we would just --

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:46.826
I would say "Pick
anything off the VHS wall"

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:49.295
and we would just smoke hookah
and then watch like

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.863
"Rainbow Brite"
or something like that.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.298
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:56.235
And that was the only thing
that could make me feel good.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.737
-Yeah.
Last time you were here,

00:03:58.737 --> 00:04:01.974
you brought a bag full of VHS
tapes just to give us

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:05.545
a sense of the flavor
of your work, of your library.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.246
-Yes.
-As you like to say.

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:08.714
-Yes, yeah.

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.015
-So I'm going to just
pull these out.

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:12.017
Thank you
for sending these over.

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:14.053
This is the perfect bag for them
to be in.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.521
-I will say, Seth,
just so you --

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:18.057
most of my tape--
the tapes you saw in that photo,

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:21.728
those have all been moved
to Los Angeles, basically.

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:25.564
So now my apartment
in New York is empty.

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:29.235
But I will open up
drawers or cabinets

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:32.538
and still find
leftover piles of VHS.

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:37.543
There's just too -- There's
too many, and it's always --

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:40.646
It doesn't make me feel great
about who I am,

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:43.883
but I wanted to show you
what I have and truly,

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:46.952
like, this was on Tuesday night
I opened up my cabinet

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:48.054
and these are some
of the things I found.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:50.823
-Alright, so tell us
a little bit about

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.292
"The Miracle of the Staircase."

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:55.127
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:56.929
-Yeah, that's just
a really good movie

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:59.232
about that spiral staircase.

00:04:59.232 --> 00:05:01.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:05.271
-The cover is kind of
giving a lot of it away?

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:07.974
-Yes. And so I do feel bad
for the --

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.776
Maybe you should
put it away now, because yeah,

00:05:10.776 --> 00:05:12.511
once you get to the...

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:15.448
It's a good movie, guys, and
you're going to want to see it.

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:17.116
[ Laughter ]
-This one. Alright.

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.784
Well, so this one,
this is, I guess,

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:22.788
an instructional video.
This is -- What's this one here?

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:24.490
-"Kittens to Cats."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.226
Again, something I feel
like everybody already knows.

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:30.429
-Yeah.
I want everybody to picture

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:33.866
who you think the woman
who stars in this is

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:36.269
and then tell me
if you thought it was her.

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:40.606
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:43.542
-Ann Childers.
-Ann Childers.

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:47.246
-One of the greats.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:49.581
-Ooh, now, I feel like
there's got to be some --

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:52.685
Tell me a little bit more
about "Kick Time!"

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:54.887
-"Kick Time!"
Fun and fitness with

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.856
Kirby the Clown
and the "Kick Time" kids.

00:05:57.856 --> 00:05:59.692
-Yeah, he is a real clown.

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:01.493
I mean,
not just a clown in behavior

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:04.229
like, a full child clown.
It seems like I wouldn't --

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:06.599
I don't know if I'd want
to learn kicking from a clown.

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:08.634
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:09.836
-Kirby's different.

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:13.539
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:16.008
-Oh, this is good,
because this seems to be

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:20.413
if your kid is hyper-specific
in their wants.

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:23.449
They probably wouldn't even
believe this exists as a film.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:24.850
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.019
-"I Wanna Be a Ship Captain."

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:30.022
This was --
So, you say hyper-specific,

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:32.257
but for me,
like every other kid

00:06:32.257 --> 00:06:35.862
I went to elementary school with
was like, "Hey."

00:06:35.862 --> 00:06:37.262
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.262 --> 00:06:39.432
"We got to do it."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:41.934
This was the goal.
-Right.

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:45.204
It's like one of us can't afford
a ship, but if we chip in...

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:46.605
[ Laughter ]

00:06:46.605 --> 00:06:49.141
-That's exactly what I said.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.141 --> 00:06:50.609
-Oh, my gosh.

00:06:50.609 --> 00:06:53.779
Now, I can't believe what this
is promising is even possible

00:06:53.779 --> 00:06:55.748
because this is telling you
they can teach you a thing

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:57.316
in five minutes.

00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:59.084
Kyle, you've obviously
had five minutes.

00:06:59.084 --> 00:07:01.721
Does this mean that you can do
what this tape says you can do?

00:07:03.221 --> 00:07:06.725 line:0%
-Yes, I can do the Macarena
and I can also get mad.

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:08.094
-You also what?

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:11.296
-Can get mad!
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.296 --> 00:07:14.734
I like -- Now, if you --
What does the front say there?

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:17.203
-The front says,
"Learn the Macarena

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:19.605
in five minutes with Yazmina."

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.673
-Yeah, so I was --
So, if you look at the back,

00:07:21.673 --> 00:07:24.910
I noticed this
one thing about it.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.347
Can you read the portion
where Yazmina is referenced?

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.616
-Yeah, sure. Uh...

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:32.618
"It's infectious and sexy.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:36.188
In this video, model
and dance instructor Yazmina

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:37.456
will show you the basic moves

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:40.860
and then move on
to some fun variations."

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.828
-Yeah, I just -- I don't know
if you can see it,

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:45.597
but Yazmina is in quotes
on the back.

00:07:45.597 --> 00:07:47.433
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:47.433 --> 00:07:48.834
-So I don't know if this is

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:51.136
what we need to know
about Yazmina.

00:07:51.136 --> 00:07:53.972
It seems like on the front,
she's presented as

00:07:53.972 --> 00:07:56.308
the Macarena queen we all know.

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:58.610
But on the back, we're like...

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.045
You know?
-Are you saying maybe

00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:02.448
on the day of, the real Yazmina
didn't show?

00:08:02.448 --> 00:08:04.449
[ Laughter ]

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:07.553
-I'm not saying anything.

00:08:07.553 --> 00:08:08.788
-Can I tell --
There's two other things

00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:10.990
I just noticed on the back.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.524
It says, "As a special bonus,

00:08:12.524 --> 00:08:14.927
Yazmina also
demonstrates the steps

00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.862
for the --" in quotes --
"'Dirty Macarena.'"

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:18.064
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:20.299
-A sexy new version
that's on the upswing.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:22.000
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.869
-Yeah, we're still --

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:25.271
Everybody's still doing
that one.

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:27.340
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, you're right.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.142
The Macarena was like a real,
you know, flash in the pan.

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:31.510
But the Dirty Macarena,
I feel like,

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.346
had some real staying power.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:34.346 --> 00:08:37.149
-Seth, when you just talk about
it, I kind of...

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:39.484
Images come into my head that,

00:08:39.484 --> 00:08:42.021
you know, maybe aren't
appropriate for the audience.

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:44.490
[ Laughter ]

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.593
-Hey, there are two things
I wanted to ask you about

00:08:47.593 --> 00:08:49.962
because again, they really
seem true to the era.

00:08:49.962 --> 00:08:52.564
The kids' names,
the twins' names are Skip.

00:08:52.564 --> 00:08:54.667
That one seems pretty
on the nose.

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:56.802
And then Treybor.
-Right.

00:08:56.802 --> 00:09:01.206
-Were you very happy
when you came up with Treybor?

00:09:01.206 --> 00:09:05.110
-Yeah. I mean, like, I think
it started as Skip and Trey,

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:09.615
and then it was truly just like,
"Maybe it's Treybor?"

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:13.919
And then it was like, "Yep, it's
going to be Skip and Treybor."

00:09:13.919 --> 00:09:15.287
-It was like,
"Maybe it's Treybor

00:09:15.287 --> 00:09:17.089
and we all call it a day?"

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:18.557
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:18.557 --> 00:09:21.026
-Also the catchphrase
"za-zi-zas,"

00:09:21.026 --> 00:09:24.296
that really felt
like '90s morning TV to me.

00:09:24.296 --> 00:09:27.132
-Zuh-zi-zas.
-Oh, sorry, I apologize.

00:09:27.132 --> 00:09:28.600
[ Laughter ]
-No, it's cool.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.268
The Internet's not going
to be happy about that.

00:09:30.268 --> 00:09:32.605
But you're fine.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:35.941
Zuh-zi-zas, yeah,
that's one of Skip's things,

00:09:35.941 --> 00:09:40.145
and, like, we just wanted
to come up with

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:43.649
the best phrase
and that was our attempt.

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:45.217
[ Laughter ]
I don't think anyone's

00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:48.454
going to argue with zuh-zi-zas
being the best phrase.

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:50.589
Hey, congrats again.
-Maybe Yazmina.

00:09:50.589 --> 00:09:54.393
-Yeah. Oh! Yazmina!

00:09:54.393 --> 00:09:55.928
Hey, thank you so much
for being here, man.

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:57.930
The show is fantastic.
Congratulations.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.632
-Thanks so much, buddy.
Take care.

00:10:00.632 --> 00:10:02.334
-Kyle Mooney, everybody.

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:03.769
"Saturday Morning
All Star Hits!"

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:05.270
is streaming now on Netflix,

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.239
and don't miss "Saturday Night
Live" this weekend.

